<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Taexali</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of north-east Scotland recorded by Ptolemy, who named the headland of Kinnaird Head after them and listed their town Devana.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Ptolemy's Geography</a> of about 150 places the Taexali in the north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a>, with a headland, Taexalorum Promontorium, usually identified as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinnaird-head">Kinnaird Head</a>, and a settlement called Devana, perhaps near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-dee-aberdeenshire">Dee</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-don-aberdeenshire">Don</a>. They grew from local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> farming communities of the lowlands between the Dee and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-deveron">Deveron</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is recorded of their rulers. The region is rich in hillforts, souterrains and recumbent stone circles from earlier ages, and Roman marching camps, such as those at Raedykes and Normandykes, show that Roman armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Severus</a> passed through their lands. Mixed farming on fertile lowland soils supported a relatively dense population.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Taexali disappear from the record after Ptolemy. Their territory later formed part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts</a>, whose symbol stones are especially numerous in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taexali.jpg" alt="Taexali" width="320" height="464"><figcaption>Image: (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain.north.peoples.Ptolemy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic (Pictish)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Taezali</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=taexali" aria-label="Taexali on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4669 302 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taexali lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4989 558h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4989 558h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=taexali">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

